lmfback wrote:
> This probably ends up as a YANI thread sooner or later but I'm curious 
> why people think Gehennom in general is so boring (I do too, don't 
> worry :-). I spent some time thinking about how it could be different 
> (without changing too much) and in the end I'm convinced it's pretty 
> cleverly thought out as it is actually.

I currently have two characters who have cleared the castle and are 
waiting to proceed, and I haven't played them for months. I'm completely 
bored of them.

I think this shows that it's not necessarily the repetitative of the 
maze which makes it boring, it's this combined with the fact that by 
this point in the game your player is most likely a walking fortress.

I guess the best way to solve it would be to either a) make it harder to 
become so strong, or b) make this stage of the game harder even if you 
this powerful. And you could also fix the mazes to make the actual 
layout more iteresting as well.

The first I think could be achieved by making wands of wishing only have 
one charge before they need to be recharged. This means you could get 
only three wishes out of the castle wand, which is going to make it 
harder to get a full ascention kit before you enter Gehennom (if you 
think it's unfair to take away the other wishes you could always put 
another wand somewhere further down, in the Sanctum for example).

You could also possibly remove the amulet of reflection from sokoban, 
although this would probably affect the middle game more than this part.

To make Gehennom harder, I guess you'd have to make the monsters which 
usually reside there tougher, by giving them more hitpoints or making 
them deal out more damage. Or maybe, give them more hitpoints only when 
they're in hell, like Air Elementals on their plane.

And the level layouts coud be much more interesting as well. I like the 
idea of more lava areas (after all, it is menat to be hell! You could 
make it even more interesting by having: "The heat of the lava melts 
your ring of levitation..."), and more open areas so monsters can 
surround you. Lots of levels which are completely different from the 
maze idea. Maybe some like the Gnomish mines, or the big room, or 
similar to some of the quest levels. But there would still need to be 
some maze levels.


> 2) No teleporting on far too many levels. I don't mind not being able 
> to teleport into certain structures (wizard's tower, Orcustown etc) but 
> I would love to be able to teleport from elsewhere on the level to 
> elsewhere on the level. That would speed up the no-teleport levels 
> nicely without breaking things.

No. I completely disagree with this.

You want the levels to allow teleporting, so that you can skip right 
past them. Why would you want to do that? Because the levels are boring.
Allowing teleporting doesn't solve the problem.

If you make the levels more interesting and challenging, people won't 
want to skip past them, so the levels can *all* be non-teleport. (This 
also means that when Rodney and his chums have you surrounded, you can't 
just teleport to the stairs to escape.)


The mysterious force is really evil. It just makes this part of the game 
tedious. I don't think it should be taken away completely however, 
instead I think it would be more interesting if it was given to Rodney 
as one of his attacks. He shouldn't use it too often, but when he does, 
he'd send you back a few levels, and rodney and any nasty monsters 
standing next to you should come along too.


